  - Sierra Nevada
  - habitat mapping

abstract: >-
  Desktop application for the automatic identification of borreguiles (high-mountain
  wet meadows) using multi-source remote sensing and
  machine learning. It combines Sentinel-2 time series (vegetation, moisture and water
  indices), Sentinel-1 radar, topographic variables derived from the Copernicus digital
  elevation model (elevation, slope and curvature) and texture descriptors from
  high-resolution orthophotography. A Random Forest classifier, trained on 769 verified
  points in Sierra Nevada (Spain; 372 borreguiles and 397 non-borreguiles) and validated
  with spatial cross-validation by river catchment, estimates the probability that each
  point is a borreguil. With the reviewed points, it also trains a model for the study
  area, validates it by zones and produces a 10 m borreguil map in Google Earth Engine.
  The program also includes a hierarchical classification of borreguil type (environment,
  moisture and pixel purity), automatic selection of the decision threshold and the
  option to retrain the model with local field data. It is distributed as a
  self-contained executable for Windows, macOS and Linux, with no need to install Python
  or any dependencies.
